Return exactly one assignment per required future work bullet. Every selected
destination must receive its exact required count. Assignments must be distinct,
collectively useful, and ordered by destination relevance.

Return ONLY:
{
  "assignments": [
    {
      "assignment_id": "allocation-1",
      "destination_work_id": "work[0]",
      "source_fact_ids": ["projects[2].highlights[0]"],
      "matched_requirements": ["supported JD requirement"],
      "intended_accomplishment": "specific STAR accomplishment to communicate",
      "inferred_impact": {
        "inference_id": "impact-1",
        "destination_work_id": "work[0]",
        "source_fact_ids": ["projects[2].highlights[0]"],
        "inference_basis": "why this exact point estimate is professionally plausible",
        "final_claim": "reduced processing time by 35%"
      }
    }
  ]
}

Rules:
- Use only source_fact_ids present in GLOBAL_MASTER_EVIDENCE. Never create an ID.
- Evidence may come from ANY work role, project, or skill group and may be
  assigned to ANY selected work destination. This cross-entry attribution is
  intentional. The destination employer, title, dates, and URL are never changed.
- Each assignment represents one future bullet and must have at least one fact.
- Prefer verified metrics already present in source facts.
- When useful evidence has no verified metric, you MAY create one plausible,
  conservative point estimate. It must contain an exact-looking number, not a
  range and not words such as approximately/about/roughly. Record its basis and
  final claim in inferred_impact. Otherwise set inferred_impact to null.
- For inferred_impact, repeat the assignment's destination_work_id and the exact
  same source_fact_ids so traceability is complete.
- Do not infer employers, titles, dates, schools, degrees, URLs, technologies
  absent from the full master, or accomplishments unrelated to the source facts.
- Return only the JSON object.
